WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.795
A search tree finds

2
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.427
inserts and deletes in time proportional

3
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:08.350
to its height.

4
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.010
So the whole question is

5
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.340
how tall is the tree?

6
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.240
Let's do an experiment.

7
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.160
We insert the keys one, two, three

8
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.940
four and so on, in increasing order

9
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.980
into an empty search tree.

10
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.850
What does the tree look like?

11
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.680
This is the last video of the chapter

12
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.870
and it ends with a problem.

13
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.737
We'll see how a search tree

14
00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:31.950
turns into a list

15
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.045
how many comparisons that costs

16
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.823
and what happens when the keys

17
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.860
arrive in random order.

18
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.070
Insert ten into an empty tree.

19
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.030
It becomes the root.

20
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.310
Insert twenty.

21
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.430
It is larger

22
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.520
so it goes to the right of ten.

23
00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.760
Insert thirty.

24
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.500
Larger than ten, larger than twenty

25
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.440
so it goes to the right of twenty.

26
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.440
Forty goes to the right of thirty

27
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.630
and so on.

28
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.056
Every new key is larger than

29
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.450
everything in the tree

30
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.730
so it always goes to the far right.

31
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.900
The left subtrees stay empty.

32
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.980
The tree is a chain.

33
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.200
Every node has just one child

34
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.620
and the tree is as tall as it can be

35
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.080
with n keys, the height is n minus one.

36
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.290
This is a linked list, drawn at an angle.

37
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.135
And it is still a perfectly

38
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.290
valid search tree.

39
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.710
The rule holds.

40
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.840
It simply is not a fast one.

41
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.481
You may have heard that a binary search

42
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.200
tree searches in time log n.

43
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.560
That is only true when the tree is short.

44
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.730
The truth is time proportional

45
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.752
to the height

46
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.251
and the height can be anywhere from

47
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.670
log base two of n to n minus one.

48
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.555
What does that cost?

49
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.631
Take a thousand keys inserted

50
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.224
in increasing order.

51
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.201
The tree has height nine

52
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.730
hundred ninety-nine.

53
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.808
To find the last key

54
00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.521
the search compares it with every

55
00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.310
one of the thousand keys.

56
00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.210
A thousand comparisons

57
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.960
where binary search would need about ten.

58
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.380
With ten thousand keys

59
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.140
the height is nine thousand

60
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.469
nine hundred ninety-nine

61
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.409
and the search for the last key makes

62
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.960
ten thousand comparisons.

63
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.485
The average node sits almost five

64
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.310
thousand steps below the root.

65
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.890
Inserting is just as bad

66
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.350
and so is deleting.

67
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.570
Every operation is as slow as a linked list.

68
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.410
We built all this machinery

69
00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.300
and got nothing.

70
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.250
Now shuffle the same thousand keys

71
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.520
and insert them in random order.

72
00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.934
The first key becomes the root

73
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.112
and it is probably neither very

74
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.480
small nor very large

75
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.911
so the tree splits roughly in two

76
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.251
and the same happens again

77
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.750
at every level below.

78
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.470
The result: a height of twenty-two

79
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.437
instead of nine hundred ninety-nine.

80
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.911
A perfectly balanced tree with a thousand

81
00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:54.901
keys would have height nine

82
00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.067
so the shuffled tree is more than

83
00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.930
twice as tall as a perfect one

84
00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.860
and still tiny next to a chain.

85
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.157
A better measure is the average

86
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.387
depth of a node.

87
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.396
For the shuffled thousand keys

88
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.657
it is eleven point oh two.

89
00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.485
Probability theory says that for random insertion

90
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.705
order the average depth is about two times

91
00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.560
the natural logarithm of n

92
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.134
minus a small constant.

93
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.155
For a thousand keys that works

94
00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.450
out to about eleven.

95
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.910
With ten thousand keys

96
00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.844
the shuffled tree has height twenty-nine

97
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.911
and the average node sits fourteen

98
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.010
point seven two steps down

99
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.321
not five thousand.

100
00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.244
Searching for every key takes about fifteen

101
00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.880
point seven comparisons on average.

102
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.160
So when keys arrive in random order

103
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.340
the tree stays short

104
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.864
and operations cost about log n.

105
00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.599
The height still grows only

106
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.136
in proportion to log n

107
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.178
though the tallest path is a bit

108
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.950
longer than the average one.

109
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.894
Why can't we just rely on random order?

110
00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.034
Because the order in which keys

111
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.010
arrive is not ours to choose.

112
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.767
Timestamps, order numbers and identifiers

113
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.856
arrive in increasing order.

114
00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.226
A list that was sorted once and then

115
00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.552
loaded into a tree is sorted.

116
00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.218
Nearly sorted data is nearly

117
00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.440
as bad as sorted data.

118
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.801
We could shuffle everything first

119
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.198
but that only works when we have

120
00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.968
all the keys in advance.

121
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.681
Many programs add and remove

122
00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:25.830
keys all the time.

123
00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.040
What we want is a tree that

124
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.680
keeps itself short

125
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.360
whatever the order of the keys.

126
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.820
After every insertion and deletion

127
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.620
it repairs its own shape

128
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.250
using only a few local changes.

129
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.210
Those local changes are called rotations

130
00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.830
and trees that use them have names

131
00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.250
AVL trees, two-four trees

132
00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:47.310
and red-black trees.

133
00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.710
They guarantee a height of order log n

134
00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.910
so search

135
00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.290
insert and delete all take time O of log n

136
00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.280
always.

137
00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.670
That is the subject of Chapter six.

138
00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.340
To sum up.

139
00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.051
Search

140
00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.815
insert and delete in a binary search tree

141
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.610
take time proportional to its height.

142
00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.554
Keys inserted in sorted order give

143
00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.650
a chain of height n minus one

144
00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.789
which is as slow as a linked list.

145
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.509
Random order gives a height

146
00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.240
proportional to log n.

147
00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.680
Real data is often sorted

148
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.300
so we need trees that balance themselves.

149
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.070
See you in Chapter six.

150
00:05:25.697 --> 00:05:28.207
Now let's run the experiment in Java

151
00:05:28.207 --> 00:05:29.417
with real numbers.

152
00:05:29.997 --> 00:05:32.467
To measure the height without recursion

153
00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:35.870
we go level by level: start with the root

154
00:05:35.947 --> 00:05:37.416
replace the level by the list

155
00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:39.707
of all the children, and count

156
00:05:39.707 --> 00:05:41.557
until the list is empty.

157
00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:44.512
The average depth uses the same loop.

158
00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.659
We avoid recursion here for a reason

159
00:05:46.717 --> 00:05:48.107
you will see in a moment.

160
00:05:48.687 --> 00:05:51.856
For the random order we need a shuffle.

161
00:05:51.937 --> 00:05:53.347
Collections dot shuffle with

162
00:05:53.397 --> 00:05:55.176
a seeded Random works

163
00:05:55.257 --> 00:05:56.610
but it would give a different

164
00:05:56.657 --> 00:05:58.367
order than the Python video.

165
00:05:58.717 --> 00:06:01.522
So we use the same tiny generator

166
00:06:01.607 --> 00:06:02.895
in both languages

167
00:06:02.967 --> 00:06:06.407
a sixty-four-bit linear congruential generator.

168
00:06:06.407 --> 00:06:07.937
In Java

169
00:06:08.107 --> 00:06:10.501
long arithmetic wraps around exactly

170
00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:12.187
like the generator expects

171
00:06:12.187 --> 00:06:15.227
and the unsigned shift picks the high bits.

172
00:06:15.227 --> 00:06:16.967
The order is identical

173
00:06:16.967 --> 00:06:18.717
so all the numbers match.

174
00:06:19.297 --> 00:06:21.103
The experiment builds a tree

175
00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:22.467
from the sorted keys

176
00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:24.507
and a tree from the shuffled keys

177
00:06:24.507 --> 00:06:27.297
for a thousand and ten thousand keys.

178
00:06:27.647 --> 00:06:30.387
Sorted: height nine hundred ninety-nine

179
00:06:30.457 --> 00:06:33.597
and nine thousand, nine hundred ninety-nine.

180
00:06:33.597 --> 00:06:36.327
Shuffled: twenty-two and twenty-nine.

181
00:06:36.677 --> 00:06:40.207
Average depth: five hundred against eleven

182
00:06:40.207 --> 00:06:43.197
and five thousand against fifteen.

183
00:06:43.777 --> 00:06:46.166
Finding the last key in the sorted tree

184
00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:48.287
takes a thousand comparisons

185
00:06:48.287 --> 00:06:50.337
and ten thousand for the bigger tree.

186
00:06:50.687 --> 00:06:53.011
In the shuffled tree a search takes

187
00:06:53.077 --> 00:06:55.060
about twelve comparisons on average

188
00:06:55.117 --> 00:06:56.417
for a thousand keys

189
00:06:56.417 --> 00:06:59.247
and about sixteen for ten thousand.

190
00:06:59.827 --> 00:07:01.333
Now the recursion.

191
00:07:01.417 --> 00:07:03.225
The recursive height method handles

192
00:07:03.277 --> 00:07:05.037
a chain of five hundred nodes.

193
00:07:05.037 --> 00:07:07.497
On a chain of a million nodes

194
00:07:07.497 --> 00:07:10.187
the same code throws a StackOverflowError.

195
00:07:10.537 --> 00:07:13.347
Java's stack is bigger than Python's limit

196
00:07:13.347 --> 00:07:15.546
so it survives thousands of levels

197
00:07:15.607 --> 00:07:18.353
but a chain is exactly the case where

198
00:07:18.427 --> 00:07:20.507
depth equals the number of keys.

199
00:07:20.507 --> 00:07:23.297
The iterative version has no such limit.

200
00:07:23.877 --> 00:07:25.227
Common mistakes.

201
00:07:25.227 --> 00:07:27.949
First, assuming that a search tree

202
00:07:28.027 --> 00:07:29.877
is automatically fast.

203
00:07:30.227 --> 00:07:33.577
Second, testing only with random data.

204
00:07:33.577 --> 00:07:36.077
Insert sorted keys in a test

205
00:07:36.077 --> 00:07:37.907
and look at the height.

206
00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:39.054
Third

207
00:07:39.187 --> 00:07:41.421
writing the recursive version of a tree algorithm

208
00:07:41.467 --> 00:07:44.217
and trusting it on any input.

209
00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.812
This is exactly why TreeMap

210
00:07:46.887 --> 00:07:48.368
and TreeSet are balanced.

211
00:07:48.427 --> 00:07:50.132
Their operations are guaranteed

212
00:07:50.187 --> 00:07:52.387
to take time O of log n

213
00:07:52.387 --> 00:07:54.247
whatever the order of the keys

214
00:07:54.247 --> 00:07:56.717
because they are red-black trees.

215
00:07:57.067 --> 00:07:59.290
Insert a million sorted keys into

216
00:07:59.357 --> 00:08:01.817
a TreeMap and it stays fast.

217
00:08:01.817 --> 00:08:03.857
Do the same with our tree

218
00:08:03.857 --> 00:08:06.667
and it becomes a million-node list.

219
00:08:07.247 --> 00:08:09.837
In Java: measure height with a loop

220
00:08:09.837 --> 00:08:11.597
test with sorted input

221
00:08:11.597 --> 00:08:14.687
and use TreeMap when you need a sorted map.

222
00:08:15.037 --> 00:08:16.787
See you in Chapter six

223
00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:18.637
where we learn how it stays balanced.

